## The ladder's fourth rung: libcurl
#
# Author instruction, and it earned its place in the first minute.
# SQLite is pure C with plain `i64` returns; libcurl is where the boundary meets
# the two things §4.19 had only argued about.
#
# **A variadic.** `curl_easy_setopt(CURL *, CURLoption, ...)` takes its value
# through `...`, which is where Apple ARM64 passes arguments on the stack rather
# than in registers (panel 013 named it; this is the first Heroes program to
# depend on it). Heroes has no user variadics, and it does not need any. Until
# 2026-11-10 the declaration named the arguments this call site passes, and
# clang checked the ones before the `...` against the real prototype; past it
# nothing held the value to a type, and a binding declares no parameter there
# since panel 243's R1 (defect 2421). So `curl_bound.h` defines the door,
# `net_set_url`, a function of a new name with a fixed prototype that calls
# `curl_easy_setopt` for the one option this program sets, and the group
# binds that: clang holds the URL to its `const char *`.
#
# **An enum return.** `CURLcode` is an `enum`, and `_Generic` selects on an
# enum's own type rather than on `i64` — so the return-type assertion this
# compiler writes refused *every enum-returning C function in existence* until
# this program was compiled. Invisible against SQLite, which returns plain `i64`.
# The repair is in `emit/decls.rs`: unary `+` applies the integer promotions, and
# the accepted set is every signed integer plus every unsigned one narrower than
# 64 bits — which is exactly what fits in Heroes' `i64` without losing a value,
# and still leaves `size_t` firing.
#
# **Enum constants**, since M-header-constants. `CURLOPT_URL` is a
# `CURLoption` enumerator, so this program named it by hand as `10002` — with the
# `CURLOPTTYPE_STRINGPOINT + 2` derivation in a comment above it and nothing on
# either side checking the arithmetic. `heroes mutate` measured that: five copied
# constants in `examples/`, none caught by anything. They became group members
# with no value, so the digits are not in this file to be wrong; and since the
# door, `CURLOPT_URL` is written in `curl_bound.h`'s C, where clang reads it,
# and the two codes this program compares stay members here.
#
# No request is made. A test that reaches the network is a test that fails on a
# train, and everything interesting here is settled before the first byte.
#
# **The group names `curl_bound.h`, beside this file, and not `curl/curl.h`**
# (defect 2229, 2026-10-26). libcurl 8.18 makes `curl_easy_setopt` a macro whose
# expansion holds a `_Pragma`, which changes how clang judges every line after
# the call, so the compiler refuses to bind it (`ffi_macro_name`); the header
# includes `curl/curl.h` and takes that macro off, as the refusal's note says,
# and the name binds the function libcurl declares.

extern "curl_bound.h" link "curl"
    # The codes this program compares libcurl's answers with. Their numbers are
    # in `curl.h` and nowhere here, which is the whole point, and so is the
    # option the door sets, `CURLOPTTYPE_STRINGPOINT + 2`. This file used to say
    # `10002`, with that derivation in a comment and nothing checking either
    # (panel 038; `heroes mutate` scored it at 5 sites, 0 caught).
    constant CURLE_OK: i64
    constant CURLE_UNSUPPORTED_PROTOCOL: i64
    function curl_version() -> cstr
    # The HANDLE, since 2026-09-13 (panel 145). `CURL` is `typedef void CURL;`
    # on this Mac's SDK — a struct only under `CURL_STRICTER` — so there is no
    # `struct CURL` to name, and this is exactly the case the completeness
    # critic raised against the `struct <tag>` spelling every seat had proposed.
    # The form writes the tag VERBATIM and starred, so `Curl` is `CURL *` and
    # the binding needs nothing else.
    record Curl tag CURL
    function curl_easy_init() -> Curl acquires curl_easy_cleanup
    # The door (`curl_bound.h`): `curl_easy_setopt` for `CURLOPT_URL`, at a
    # fixed prototype. libcurl copies the string, so the URL is lent.
    function net_set_url(handle: Curl, url: cstr lent) -> i64
    function curl_easy_strerror(code: u32) -> cstr
    function curl_easy_cleanup(handle: Curl consumes)

function main()
    print("libcurl: ", validated(c: curl_version()).default("<unnamed>"))
    handle = curl_easy_init()

    # The variadic call, through the door. `CURLOPT_URL` takes a string, and
    # that is a fact about this option rather than about the function, which
    # is exactly what a variadic means and why the boundary cannot check it
    # past the `...`: the door is a function per option, so the fact is its
    # prototype's. Until 2026-11-10 this line handed `curl_easy_setopt` the
    # option as `CURLOPT_URL.to_u32().must()` and the string as a LEASE,
    # because whether libcurl keeps the pointer is a fact about the option;
    # the door names the option, and libcurl copies every string option but
    # `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS`, so the string is lent for the call alone (spec
    # § 13, panel 171).
    rc = net_set_url(handle, "https://example.invalid/".cstr())

    if rc == CURLE_OK
        print("the url was accepted")
    else
        print("setopt refused it: ", validated(c: curl_easy_strerror(rc.to_u32().must())).default("<unnamed>"))

    # A code the library knows and this program never provokes: the point is
    # that the message comes from libcurl, not from a table written here.
    print(
        "code 1 means: ",
        validated(c: curl_easy_strerror(CURLE_UNSUPPORTED_PROTOCOL.to_u32().must())).default("<unnamed>")
    )
    curl_easy_cleanup(handle)
